<figure class="method-figure" aria-labelledby="spatial-title">
  <figcaption id="spatial-title"><span>Figure 3</span> Cover values, or partition their space</figcaption>
  <p class="figure-query">Find points inside the box from (0, 0) to (4, 4)</p>
  <div class="spatial-pair">
    <div><p class="panel-label">GiST · bounding regions</p><svg viewBox="0 0 260 230" role="img" aria-label="Two bounding regions contain the same four points. The lower region intersects the query box. The upper region does not and is skipped."><rect class="query" x="20" y="115" width="110" height="100"/><rect class="region" x="47" y="140" width="56" height="50"/><rect class="region skip" x="74" y="65" width="138" height="25"/><circle cx="47" cy="190" r="5"/><circle cx="103" cy="140" r="5"/><circle cx="74" cy="65" r="5"/><circle cx="212" cy="90" r="5"/><text x="30" y="132">follow</text><text x="110" y="53">skip this region</text><text x="20" y="229">(0, 0)</text><text x="135" y="125">(4, 4)</text></svg><p>A child summary covers its values. If its region cannot meet the box, skip the whole child. Bounding regions may overlap.</p></div>
    <div><p class="panel-label">SP-GiST · space partitions</p><svg viewBox="0 0 260 230" role="img" aria-label="The same four points in four quadrants split at x equals four, y equals four. The lower left partition intersects the query interior; the two lower points are returned."><rect class="query" x="20" y="115" width="110" height="100"/><path class="partition" d="M130 15V215 M20 115H240"/><circle cx="47" cy="190" r="5"/><circle cx="103" cy="140" r="5"/><circle cx="74" cy="65" r="5"/><circle cx="212" cy="90" r="5"/><text x="26" y="47">skip</text><text x="165" y="47">skip</text><text x="165" y="150">skip</text><text x="30" y="132">follow</text><text x="20" y="229">(0, 0)</text><text x="135" y="125">(4, 4)</text></svg><p>A split assigns values to regions of space. Follow the partitions the box can reach, applying the rule again inside each.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="figure-note">Conceptual pruning on points (1,1), (3,3), (2,6) and (7,5). The box returns the first two. Rectangles and split positions are chosen to show the distinction; they are not a dump of PostgreSQL pages. Boundary handling belongs to the class; no point lies on this drawing’s split.</p>
</figure>
